// The company registry: what sector an employer trades in, and how many people
// work there.
//
// ── What this file is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// GENERATED, then hand-corrected. It is the only place the company filters get
// their facts, because neither fact is on a job posting: a posting says what
// the job is, never what the company is. `lib/segments.mjs` defines what the
// values mean and CONTRIBUTING.md says how to add one — it is a two-line pull
// request and the most useful one anybody can send this repository.
//
// ── The rule ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **A company nobody could identify is absent, never guessed.** An absent
// employer appears in the main list and in every field, role and location
// filter exactly as before; it is simply in no company-type filter. A WRONG one
// files an employer under a sector it is not in, on a page a reader chose
// deliberately, and it is the reason they stop trusting the other filters. So
// `null` on either field is a legitimate answer, an entry with nothing known is
// not written at all, and a sector is never inferred from a company's name —
// "Fable Security" need not be a security company and "Roshal Health" is not a
// hospital.
//
// Size is a BAND rather than a number because headcount moves: "1,000–9,999
// people" survives a year of it and "4,300" does not.
//
// The same registry, with the same keys, lives in the sibling job list and in
// the question bank (`scripts/company_registry.py` in Tech-Interview-Questions).
// A company's sector must not depend on which repository you read it in.
//
// 1,103 employers, 1,099 with a sector and 781 with a headcount band.

